# Commit Message Format

Default: a short imperative **title only** (no trailing period). Focus on why/what landed, not a file laundry list.

Use a **blank line + bullets** only when the commit includes several distinct updates that cannot be summed up in one sentence. One or two related changes that fit in the title do not need a body.

## Rules

- **TITLE**: Short imperative summary. Do not wrap it in quotes or prefix with `feat:` / `fix:` unless the repo already uses that convention in recent commits.
- **Bullets** (optional): Only for truly multi-change commits. One concrete change per line; start with a verb when useful (Add, Fix, Update, Remove). Prefer 3–6 bullets; skip trivial or purely mechanical noise. Do not use bullets for 1–2 things that the title already covers.
- If you use bullets, put a blank line between the title and the list. If you do not, do not add extra blank lines or a body paragraph.
- Pass the full message via a HEREDOC when running `git commit`.

## Examples

Title only (typical):

```
Fix LinkedIn job listing description scrape when the panel remounts
```

Bullets (several distinct updates):

```
Add cover letter credit cost estimate API

- Add POST /api/coverLetter/cost that returns cost and discounts without charging
- Expose get_cover_letter_cost MCP tool and document public /api/coverLetter routes
- Add discount labels to creditDiscounts SSOT and use them in RegenerateAction
```
